; dtmf_gen.loom
; A DTMF (telephone touch-tone) generator: for each key the host queues, the
; sum of its low-group and high-group tones for a host-set time, then a
; host-set pause, then the next key, as a one-bit pulse-density stream on
; one pin that an RC low-pass turns into audio.
;
; Written on 2026-11-07, after the hardware was complete (D-032), as a
; protocol Loom was not designed for (D-035). DTMF is not a line code at
; all but two analog sine waves added together, and nothing in Loom was
; built for it: there is no DAC, no PWM unit, no oscillator. The program
; uses:
;   - the per-thread tick (TICK_INT, built for UART baud rates) as the
;     sample clock of a first-order sigma-delta modulator: one output bit
;     per tick, 125 000 a second;
;   - the deadline schedule (WAITD 1 every sample, proved by the checker);
;   - the data memory (LD, built for device-emulation tables such as a
;     24C02's contents) for a 64-entry sine table and the key's phase steps;
;   - OUT through a one-pin OUTGRP group, so the bit computed from the carry
;     flag goes to the pin with no branch: every sample's write lands the
;     same number of clocks after its tick.
; What the firmware does that no hardware does: two numerically controlled
; oscillators (16-bit phase accumulators), the table look-up, the two-tone
; sum, the sigma-delta modulation itself (the carry of a 16-bit
; accumulator is the output bit), the keypad-to-frequency mapping and the
; tone and pause timing.
;
; Standard: ITU-T Q.23 (11/1988) and Q.24 (11/1988), as
; tools/protomodels/dtmf.py cites them. Q.23 section 6: low group 697, 770,
; 852, 941 Hz, high group 1209, 1336, 1477, 1633 Hz, keys as Figure 1/Q.23;
; section 7.1: each frequency within +-1.8 %; section 7.2: distortion
; products at least 20 dB below the fundamentals. Q.24 Table A-1 (AT&T
; column): a receiver operates on 40 ms and more, rejects 23 ms and less,
; needs a 40 ms pause, and takes a level difference of +4 to -8 dB.
;
; Sample rate and frequencies. fs = 125 000 Hz (50 MHz / 400). A tone's
; phase step is round(f * 65536 / fs), so the frequencies made are
;     697 -> 365 = 696.18 Hz (-0.12 %)   1209 -> 634 = 1209.26 Hz (+0.02 %)
;     770 -> 404 = 770.57 Hz (+0.07 %)   1336 -> 700 = 1335.14 Hz (-0.06 %)
;     852 -> 447 = 852.59 Hz (+0.07 %)   1477 -> 774 = 1476.29 Hz (-0.05 %)
;     941 -> 493 = 940.32 Hz (-0.07 %)   1633 -> 856 = 1632.69 Hz (-0.02 %)
; the worst 0.12 %, against Q.23's 1.8 % (and against Q.24's AT&T receive
; limit of 1.5 %); a board clock off by e ppm moves them all by e ppm. The
; frequencies scale with the tick: at any other clock the host sets the
; tick to clock / 125 000 (the checker proves the loop for 144 clocks, so
; any board of 18 MHz or more).
;
; The table holds 16384 + round(16000 sin(2 pi i / 64)), i = 0..63; the top
; six bits of the phase pick the entry. Two entries added are 768..64 768,
; the modulator's input x, read as x / 65536 of full scale: the pin's mean
; level is 1/2 + 0.244 (sin a + sin b), each tone 0.244 of the logic swing
; in amplitude, equal (no twist: both tones come from one table). The
; modulator adds x to a 16-bit accumulator each sample; the carry out is
; the bit, so the density of ones is x / 65536 exactly over time.
; Silence (the pause, and while no key is queued) is x = 32768: the pin
; alternates 1, 0 at 62.5 kHz, which the filter turns into a steady half
; level, so the line does not step between keys.
;
; Spectrum: phase truncation to 6 bits puts spurs near -40 dB, and the
; first-order modulator's noise (oversampling 18x over 3.4 kHz) dominates.
; tools/protomodels/dtmf.py measured the total in 300..3400 Hz at -27 to
; -31 dB under the weaker tone for the sixteen keys, against Q.23's -20 dB.
;
; Timing. The host gives both durations in sample periods (8 us at
; 125 kHz: 125 per ms). A tone is exactly its count of samples and a pause
; exactly its count of silent samples; while the queue is empty the
; silence goes on and INQ is looked at again every sample, so the next
; tone starts on the first sample boundary after its key arrives. The
; next phase is set up in the slots after the last sample of the one
; before, inside the same sample period, so the sample grid never slips.
; Q.24 asks 40 ms (5000 samples) for both.
;
; Pins: TX = OUT0 (any output with --pin TX=<pin>). The RC filter (for
; example 1 kohm and 47 nF, a 3.4 kHz corner) and any line interface are
; the board's, not Loom's.
;
; Host interface (FIFO space, thread 0):
;   INQ, first: the tone duration in samples (1..65535, 524 ms at most),
;   INQ, second: the pause duration in samples (1..65535);
;   INQ, then: one word a key, bits 3:0 = 4 * row + column of Figure
;     1/Q.23 (0 '1', 1 '2', 2 '3', 3 'A', 4 '4', ... 12 '*', 13 '0',
;     14 '#', 15 'D'); other bits ignored.
;   Nothing goes to OUTQ.
;
; Memory: 0x000 .. 0x07F, thread 0's whole quarter (.imem 512): the code and
; the step and duration words in 0x000 .. 0x03F, the sine table in
; 0x040 .. 0x07F. The table is reached by an absolute address (ADDI 63 then
; LD +1: no base register is left), so the program runs as thread 0 only.
;
; Deadlines: the longest path from one WAITD 1 to the next is a sample that
; ends a pause and takes a key, 36 slots, 144 clocks: the checker proves a
; tick of 144 clocks (the .tick below), any board of 18 MHz or more. At
; 50 MHz the tick is 400 clocks.
;
; Readings taken on the open spec questions (2026-11-07):
;   Reception limits from the AT&T column of Q.24 Table A-1 (the tightest
;   frequency limit, 1.5 %), and Q.23 section 7 for the transmitter.
;   Durations are set in samples (125 a millisecond), not milliseconds.
;   No high-group pre-emphasis: twist is 0 dB, inside every column.
;   No detector is shipped. A one-bit input cannot meet Q.24 (the
;   tests in tools/tests/test_fw_dtmf.py record the numbers), and a
;   detector that works only at zero twist would not be a DTMF receiver.

        .imem   512
        .thread 0
        .pins   TX = OUT0
        .tick   144
        .equ    TBL = 0x40                      ; the sine table, 64 words

; r0 r1 the low and high phase (16 bits, 2^16 = one cycle)   r2 r3 their
; steps (0 in silence)   r4 the modulator's accumulator   r5 the next bit
; (and scratch before it is computed)   r6 scratch   r7 samples left
        JMP     start
LOWSTEP: .word  365, 404, 447, 493              ; 697 770 852 941 Hz
HIGHSTEP: .word 634, 700, 774, 856              ; 1209 1336 1477 1633 Hz
DUR:    .word   0, 0                            ; tone, pause (samples)

start:  .csr    OUTGRP, (1 << 5) | pin(TX)      ; OUT r writes r[0] to TX
        LDI     r0, 0                           ; silence: both phases at
        LDI     r1, 0                           ; entry 0 (sin 0), both
        LDI     r2, 0                           ; steps 0; r4 and r5 may
        LDI     r3, 0                           ; start from anything
        POP     r7                              ; the tone duration (blocks)
        ST      r7, r0, DUR
        POP     r7                              ; the pause duration (blocks)
        ST      r7, r0, DUR + 1
        LDI     r7, 1                           ; one silent sample, then INQ
        SETD    1

; One sample: the bit decided last sample goes out right after the tick,
; then both phases advance, two table words are added and the sum enters
; the modulator. 18 slots and the WAITD; no branch but the loop's own.
loop:   WAITD   1
        OUT     r5
        ADD     r0, r0, r2
        ADD     r1, r1, r3
        MOV     r5, r0
        SHRI    r5, 10                          ; the top 6 bits of the phase
        ADDI    r5, 63
        LD      r5, r5, TBL - 63                ; 16384 + 16000 sin(low)
        MOV     r6, r1
        SHRI    r6, 10
        ADDI    r6, 63
        LD      r6, r6, TBL - 63                ; 16384 + 16000 sin(high)
        ADD     r5, r5, r6                      ; x, 768 .. 64768
        ADD     r4, r4, r5                      ; C = the bit
        CSRR    r5, FLAGS                       ; {T, C, Z}
        SHRI    r5, 1                           ; r5[0] = C
        DJNZ    r7, loop

; The last sample of a tone, a pause or an idle wait has been computed (r5
; holds its bit): set up the next phase in the same sample period.
        TEST    r2, r2                          ; a tone (a step is never 0)?
        BZ      key
        LDI     r0, 0                           ; tone over: silence for the
        LDI     r1, 0                           ; pause
        LDI     r2, 0
        LDI     r3, 0
        LD      r7, r0, DUR + 1
        JMP     loop
key:    LDI     r7, 1                           ; queue empty: one more silent
        WAITB   INQ_NE, T                       ; sample. A test, not a wait:
        BT      loop                            ; TD (this sample's) is reached
        .bounded "guarded by the WAITB above; only this thread pops INQ"
        POP     r6
        MOV     r2, r6                          ; r0 = r1 = 0 here (silence)
        SHRI    r2, 2
        ANDI    r2, 3                           ; the row: low group
        LD      r2, r2, LOWSTEP
        ANDI    r6, 3                           ; the column: high group
        LD      r3, r6, HIGHSTEP
        LD      r7, r0, DUR                     ; the tone duration
        JMP     loop

        .org    TBL                             ; 16384 + round(16000 sin(2 pi i / 64))
        .word   16384, 17952, 19505, 21029, 22507, 23926, 25273, 26534
        .word   27698, 28752, 29688, 30495, 31166, 31695, 32077, 32307
        .word   32384, 32307, 32077, 31695, 31166, 30495, 29688, 28752
        .word   27698, 26534, 25273, 23926, 22507, 21029, 19505, 17952
        .word   16384, 14816, 13263, 11739, 10261,  8842,  7495,  6234
        .word    5070,  4016,  3080,  2273,  1602,  1073,   691,   461
        .word     384,   461,   691,  1073,  1602,  2273,  3080,  4016
        .word    5070,  6234,  7495,  8842, 10261, 11739, 13263, 14816
